Q: Allowing Untrusted SSL Certificates with HttpClient A: <p>If creating the <code>HttpClient</code> object using <code>SocketsHttpHandler</code>, the <code>SslOptions</code> property called <code>RemoteCertificateValidationCallback</code> can be used in the same manner as the <code>ServerCertificateValidationCallback</code> property mentioned in other answers.</p>
<p>For example, like this, to trust all remote certificates:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var handler = new SocketsHttpHandler
{
    SslOptions = new SslClientAuthenticationOptions
    {
        RemoteCertificateValidationCallback = (sender, certificate, chain, sslPolicyErrors) =&gt; true,
    }
};
var client = new HttpClient(handler);
</code></pre>
<p>Of course, it is important to make sure that the <code>HttpClient</code> created in this manner is only used to connect to well-known and trusted server endpoints.</p>

Q: PowerShell script email layout A: <p>Looks like the issue is caused by missing a loop on the events, because you may have more than one then you get all of them interpolated into a single template, so the solution might be as simple as:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$EventID = 1074, 6006, 41, 6008, 6005, 1076, 1033, 13, 6009, 12 # Your specific Event ID
$LogName = 'Application' # Or Security, System, etc.

# Get the latest event matching your criteria
$Events = Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName = $LogName; ID = $EventID } -MaxEvents 3

$Subject = &quot;Alert: Server has restarted/shutdown! - Event ID(s) $($Events.Id -join ', ')&quot;
$Body = $Events | ForEach-Object {
    @(
        &quot;Server has Restarted or Shutdown with Event ID: $($_.Id)&quot;
        &quot;Source: $($_.ProviderName)&quot;
        &quot;Message:`n$($_.Message)&quot;
    ) -join &quot;`n&quot;
}

$EmailParams = @{
    Subject    = $Subject
    Body       = $Body -join &quot;`n&quot; # $Body might be an array, join with new lines
    From       = 'email@example.com'
    To         = 'email@exaple.com'
    SmtpServer = 'your smtp address' # e.g., smtp.office365.com
}
Send-MailMessage @EmailParams
</code></pre>
<p>If you wanted to take it further, you could construct an <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/ul" rel="nofollow noreferrer">HTML list</a> with each event then include <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/send-mailmessage#-bodyashtml" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>-BodyAsHtml</code></a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$EventID = 1074, 6006, 41, 6008, 6005, 1076, 1033, 13, 6009, 12 # Your specific Event ID
$LogName = 'Application' # Or Security, System, etc.

# Get the latest event matching your criteria
$Events = Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName = $LogName; ID = $EventID } -MaxEvents 3

$Subject = &quot;Alert: Server has restarted/shutdown! - Event ID(s) $($Events.Id -join ', ')&quot;
$Body = @(
    '&lt;p&gt;Server has Restarted or Shutdown with Event ID(s):&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br&gt;'
    $Events | ForEach-Object {
        @(
            '&lt;ul&gt;'
                &quot;&lt;li&gt;Id: $($_.Id)&lt;/li&gt;&quot;
                &quot;&lt;li&gt;Source: $($_.ProviderName)&lt;/li&gt;&quot;
                &quot;&lt;li&gt;Message: $($_.Message)&lt;/li&gt;&quot;
            '&lt;/ul&gt;'
        ) -join &quot;`n&quot;
    }
)

$EmailParams = @{
    Subject    = $Subject
    Body       = $Body -join &quot;`n&quot; # $Body might be an array, join with new lines
    From       = 'email@example.com'
    To         = 'email@exaple.com'
    SmtpServer = 'your smtp address' # e.g., smtp.office365.com
    BodyAsHtml = $true # &lt;--- Need this to render HTML properly
}
Send-MailMessage @EmailParams
</code></pre>
<p>Yet another option if you want an <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/html/html_tables.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">HTML table</a> instead of a list, is to use <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/convertto-html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ConvertTo-Html</code></a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$EventID = 1074, 6006, 41, 6008, 6005, 1076, 1033, 13, 6009, 12 # Your specific Event ID
$LogName = 'Application' # Or Security, System, etc.

# Get the latest event matching your criteria
$Events = Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName = $LogName; ID = $EventID } -MaxEvents 3

$css = '
&lt;style&gt;
table, th, td {
    border: 1px solid
}
&lt;/style&gt;'

$Subject = &quot;Alert: Server has restarted/shutdown! - Event ID(s) $($Events.Id -join ', ')&quot;
$Body = $Events | Select-Object Id, @{ Name = 'Source'; Expression = 'ProviderName' }, Message |
    ConvertTo-Html -PreContent $css, '&lt;p&gt;Server has Restarted or Shutdown with Event ID(s):&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br&gt;' |
    Out-String

$EmailParams = @{
    Subject    = $Subject
    Body       = $Body
    From       = 'email@example.com'
    To         = 'email@exaple.com'
    SmtpServer = 'your smtp address' # e.g., smtp.office365.com
    BodyAsHtml = $true # &lt;--- Need this to render HTML properly
}
Send-MailMessage @EmailParams
</code></pre>
